.TH WORKSPACES 6
.SH NAME
workspaces \- which workspace a window opens on
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR Intuition (4)
reads
.B $home/lib/workspaces
first, or
.B /lib/workspaces
when there is none, at start and again on a
.B reload
line written to
.BR /srv/draw/ctl .
The file is a set of rules, one per line, naming a workspace a window
opens on by its title:
.IP
.EX
name   workspace
.EE
.PP
.I Name
is the exact text a window's title bar carries, the same string a
.B name
line writes to the window's
.IR ctl ,
described in
.IR intuition (4).
.I Workspace
is a number from one to nine.
A
.B #
starts a comment that runs to the end of the line, and a blank line is
skipped.
A line whose second word is not a number in range is skipped as well.
At most thirty-two rules are kept.
.PP
A rule is applied when a window's name is set or changed, never before:
a window is born on whatever workspace was current when its client
claimed it, and only a later
.B name
line can move it by a rule.
A window whose name matches no rule stays on the workspace it was born
on.
A window moved this way is moved exactly as a
.BI "wctl workspace " n
line would move it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
# workspaces: a window's name, and the workspace it opens on
terminal   2
view       3
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP 20
.B $home/lib/workspaces
a user's own rules, read first
.TP
.B /lib/workspaces
the machine's, read when there is no user file
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/intuition/workspace.odin
.br
.B servers/intuition/workspaces
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intuition (4),
.IR keys (6)
.SH BUGS
A rule names a window by its exact title, so a program whose title
carries a changing suffix, such as a file name, matches no rule at all.
